<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mursili II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1321–1295 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century-bce">14th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mursili II was King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> from about 1321 to 1295 BCE, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>. His annals record yearly campaigns against the Kaska in the north and against Azzi-Hayasa, and his conquest of Arzawa around 1320 BCE, after which he divided its lands among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kings bound by treaties, such as those of the Seha River Land and Hapalla. He also put down revolts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including Nuhashshe.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mursili was the fifth son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> and Queen Henti, and the younger brother of Arnuwanda II. He also had a sister and three more brothers. Two surviving elder brothers served as viceroys of Carchemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, among them Sarri-Kush. The plague that ravaged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> in the 1330s BCE killed both his father and Arnuwanda, and Mursili assumed the throne in 1321 BCE, after Arnuwanda's premature death. Dates for his reign vary with the chronology used: about 1330 to 1295 BCE in the middle chronology, or 1321 to 1295 BCE in the short chronology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a>'s enemies greeted him with contempt, since he was seen as an inexperienced ruler who had become king only through Arnuwanda's early death, and he faced numerous rebellions early in his reign. The most serious were those begun by the Kaskas in the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, by the Arzawa kingdom in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and by the Hayasa-Azzi confederation in eastern Anatolia. His Annals record the scorn of one foe: &quot;You are a child; you know nothing and instill no fear in me.&quot; Young and inexperienced as he was, he was almost certainly not a child: had he been one, other arrangements would have been made to secure the empire's stability.</p><p>The Annals for his first ten years survive. In the first two years he carried out punitive campaigns against the Kaska tribes to secure the northern borders. He then turned west to resist Uhhaziti, king of Arzawa, who was trying to lure Hittite allies into his camp. In his ninth year his cupbearer Nuvanza decisively defeated the Hayasan forces at the Battle of Ganuvara, after which the Hayasa-Azzi were reduced to Hittite vassals. The Annals also record an omen of the sun, a solar eclipse, in his tenth year, as he was about to launch his campaign against the Hayasa-Azzi.</p><p>This eclipse matters for dating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> within the chronology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>. Only two dates are possible, 24 June 1312 BCE or 13 April 1308 BCE. Trevor R. Bryce accepts the earlier, and most scholars follow, because it was near total over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> around noon, while Paul Åström suggested the later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His highest confirmed date is his twenty-second year. He is believed to have lived a few years beyond it and to have died after a reign of about 25 to 27 years, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> succeeded him. Another son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a>, also became a king. Mursili is known from many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> texts, among them the Annals, treaties with Duppi-Teššup of Amurru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niqmepa-of-ugarit">Niqmepa of Ugarit</a>, Targašnalli of Ḫapalla and Manapa-Tarḫunta of Šēḫa, decrees on the borders of Ugarit, plague prayers and hymns to the Sun Goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muršili II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Niqmaddu II’s successors included Arhalba and Niqmepa, whose long reign saw the district of Siyannu detached from Ugarit by <b>Mursili II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Mursili II</b> made a plea to the gods at a time of struggle in the land.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>In the tenth year of the Hittite king <b>Mursili II</b>, as he was about to launch a campaign against the Kingdom of Hayasa-Azzi, &quot;the Sun gave a sign&quot;: an eclipse that most scholars date to this day.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-24">24 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mursili II</b> of the Hittite Empire defeated King Uhha-ziti c. 1320 BCE and took Apasa; Uhha-ziti fled across the sea to the islands.</blockquote><a href="/state/arzawa">Arzawa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mursili II</b> fought Anniya of Azzi in long campaigns, and Hayasa disappears from the record in the 13th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/azzi-hayasa">Azzi-Hayasa</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Mursili II</b> of the Hittite Empire defeated Arzawa c. 1320 BCE, he made Targasnalli king of Hapalla.</blockquote><a href="/state/hapalla">Hapalla</a></li></ul></section>
